Save score.h with the guarded declaration and score.c with its definition below. Each .c file plus included headers forms a translation unit. Compile gcc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror main.c score.c -o app, then run ./app. A declaration alone does not provide the linked function body.
```c
#ifndef SCORE_H
#define SCORE_H
int score(int value);
#endif

/* score.c */
#include "score.h"
int score(int value) { return value * 2; }
```

value.h declares public_value, which calls a private helper in value.c. Other source files use the public operation rather than relying on the hidden helper.
```c
/* value.h */
int public_value(void);
/* value.c */
static int helper(void) { return 9; }
int public_value(void) { return helper(); }
```

With score.h and score.c from the visible definitions below, compile gcc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -c main.c score.c, then link gcc main.o score.o -o app. -c stops before linking. Omitting score.o at link time leaves the definition unresolved. A real incremental build must also rebuild affected objects when included headers change.
```c
#ifndef SCORE_H
#define SCORE_H
int score(int value);
#endif

/* score.c */
#include "score.h"
int score(int value) { return value * 2; }
```
